WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.664
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:09.582
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.882 --> 00:00:16.343
Unit twenty: Inflation and Prices. Today you will add information with relative clauses using whose and where.

4
00:00:16.643 --> 00:00:19.237
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.665
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:25.657
Inflation.

7
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.950
Inflation means a general rise in prices over time.

8
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.840
Inflation made food more expensive.

9
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.149
Your turn. Say: Inflation.

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.071
Inflation.

11
00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:41.795
Prices.

12
00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:46.281
Prices means the amounts of money asked for goods and services.

13
00:00:46.883 --> 00:00:48.836
Prices rose in every market.

14
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.928
Your turn. Say: Prices.

15
00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:55.745
Prices.

16
00:00:56.483 --> 00:00:58.193
Consumer Price Index.

17
00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:04.074
Consumer Price Index means a measure of changes in the average price of household goods.

18
00:01:04.683 --> 00:01:07.658
The Consumer Price Index rose by two percent.

19
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:12.226
Your turn. Say: Consumer Price Index.

20
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:17.008
Consumer Price Index.

21
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:18.867
Interest rate.

22
00:01:19.883 --> 00:01:23.114
Interest rate means the percentage charged for borrowing money.

23
00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:26.088
The interest rate was raised to fight inflation.

24
00:01:26.683 --> 00:01:29.533
Your turn. Say: Interest rate.

25
00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:33.507
Interest rate.

26
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.715
Purchasing power.

27
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:39.912
Purchasing power means the amount of goods your money can buy.

28
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:42.934
Inflation reduces purchasing power.

29
00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.632
Your turn. Say: Purchasing power.

30
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:50.909
Purchasing power.

31
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:52.593
Wages.

32
00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:56.221
Wages means money paid to workers for their work.

33
00:01:56.817 --> 00:01:59.257
Wages did not keep up with prices.

34
00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.084
Your turn. Say: Wages.

35
00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:05.934
Wages.

36
00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:07.673
Central bank.

37
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:12.831
Central bank means the national bank that controls money and interest rates.

38
00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:16.090
The central bank watches inflation closely.

39
00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:19.185
Your turn. Say: Central bank.

40
00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:23.220
Central bank.

41
00:02:23.983 --> 00:02:25.159
Cost of living.

42
00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:29.891
Cost of living means the amount of money needed to pay for basic needs.

43
00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:32.854
The cost of living is higher in the capital.

44
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.114
Your turn. Say: Cost of living.

45
00:02:38.967 --> 00:02:40.212
Cost of living.

46
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.628
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.483 --> 00:02:51.143
Visal, a news story says 'Families whose wages have not risen are struggling.' What does 'whose' do?

48
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:59.813
It shows possession in a relative clause. 'Families whose wages have not risen' means 'the families' wages have not risen'.

49
00:03:00.917 --> 00:03:01.906
And 'where'?

50
00:03:03.017 --> 00:03:11.323
Where refers to a place: 'The market where prices rose fastest is in the capital.' It replaces in which.

51
00:03:12.417 --> 00:03:14.451
How does this help in economic news?

52
00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:24.214
It lets reporters add detail: a central bank whose main goal is stable prices, or a country where the cost of living is soaring.

53
00:03:25.367 --> 00:03:27.111
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:31.401
Grammar focus: Relative clauses with whose and where.

55
00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:32.911
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.617 --> 00:03:36.441
Families whose wages have not risen are struggling.

57
00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.476
The market where prices rose fastest is in the capital.

58
00:03:41.383 --> 00:03:45.194
A central bank whose goal is stable prices may raise the interest rate.

59
00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:55.058
Whose + noun shows possession for people, things and organisations. Where replaces 'in which' for places. Do not add a possessive pronoun again.

60
00:03:55.817 --> 00:04:00.315
Be careful! Don't say: Families whose their wages fell.

61
00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:04.858
Say: Families whose wages fell.

62
00:04:05.158 --> 00:04:07.355
Do not repeat a possessive after whose.

63
00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:11.283
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.589
When Prices Climb.

65
00:04:13.889 --> 00:04:18.605
Inflation is a general rise in prices, and the Consumer Price Index measures it.

66
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.446
Families whose wages have not risen lose purchasing power every month.

67
00:04:24.433 --> 00:04:29.730
A central bank whose main goal is stable prices may raise the interest rate to slow inflation.

68
00:04:30.733 --> 00:04:34.474
In cities where the cost of living is highest, the pressure is greatest.

69
00:04:35.467 --> 00:04:39.126
Good policy therefore protects the people whose incomes are lowest.

70
00:04:40.233 --> 00:04:44.293
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.055
Families blank wages have not risen are struggling.

72
00:04:48.355 --> 00:04:51.811
A, whose. B, who. C, which.

73
00:04:55.367 --> 00:04:57.146
The answer is A: whose.

74
00:04:58.283 --> 00:05:01.908
The market blank prices rose fastest is in the capital.

75
00:05:02.208 --> 00:05:05.432
A, where. B, whose. C, who.

76
00:05:08.967 --> 00:05:10.479
The answer is A: where.

77
00:05:11.617 --> 00:05:14.173
The amount your money can buy is your blank power.

78
00:05:14.473 --> 00:05:17.883
A, purchasing. B, interest. C, wage.

79
00:05:21.433 --> 00:05:23.352
The answer is A: purchasing.

80
00:05:24.517 --> 00:05:26.330
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:35.009
A, A bank whose its goal is stable prices.. B, A bank whose goal is stable prices.. C, A bank which goal is stable prices..

82
00:05:38.567 --> 00:05:42.517
The answer is B: A bank whose goal is stable prices..

83
00:05:43.683 --> 00:05:47.715
According to the reading, good policy protects people whose incomes are…

84
00:05:48.015 --> 00:05:51.587
A, lowest. B, highest. C, average.

85
00:05:55.133 --> 00:05:56.819
The answer is A: lowest.

86
00:05:58.033 --> 00:06:01.162
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:02.017 --> 00:06:04.202
How does inflation affect families?

88
00:06:13.017 --> 00:06:14.494
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:14.794 --> 00:06:40.915
Inflation reduces the purchasing power of a family's money, so the same income buys less food, rent and transport. Families whose wages do not rise suffer most, and savers may also lose value. As a result, households must cut spending or borrow more. Central banks raise the interest rate to control inflation, but this can make loans more expensive.

90
00:06:41.215 --> 00:06:42.715
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:43.617 --> 00:06:48.452
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and relative clauses with whose and where.

92
00:06:48.752 --> 00:06:54.481
Today's value is economic awareness. Understand how money and work affect your life choices.

93
00:06:54.781 --> 00:06:57.445
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:06:57.745 --> 00:07:00.861
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

